  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 138 PM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nA weak cold front will wash out Thursday as it approaches the \nQuad State from the northwest, but it will lead to a surge of \nsurface high pressure across the region Friday. That will switch\nwinds to the east or southeast and lead to slightly less hot \nconditions. The warmest day of the week should be Thursday when \nthere is a 40-60% of reaching 80 degrees over the western 2/3 of\nthe area.\n\nGuidance continues to bounce around on the QPF timing and\nlocation this weekend. It looks like the heavier rain, in the\nvicinity of half an inch will hold off until late Saturday\nevening through Sunday morning. There is some meager\ninstability, so thunder has returned to portions of the region\nSaturday night and in the northeast on Sunday morning. Ensembles\nindicate that any rain over southeast Missouri Saturday\nafternoon or evening should be under a tenth of an inch. \n\nThis is a tropical remnant and the ensembles continue to \nindicate precipitable water values at or above 200% of normal \nfor this time of year. That certainly is reason to keep an eye \nout for more significant rainfall. There is a greater than 30% \nchance of 1\" or more of rainfall mainly over west Kentucky, and \neven a 5-10% chance of 2\".\n\nThis system should push east of the Quad State Sunday afternoon,\nleaving the area dry later Sunday through much of Monday. There\nis a fairly strong consensus for upper-level energy to dive \nsoutheast through the Rockies and into the Plains by Tuesday. \nThis system helps usher in a fairly amplified pattern, which \nshould allow the system to hang out for a while. There is \nconsiderable uncertainty in what latitude the system will set up\nover the Plains, which will have significant impact on when it \nwill bring precipitation to the Quad State. The NBM has small \nchance PoPs beginning Monday afternoon, with 40-50% chances \nMonday night into next Tuesday. There are some slight chances \nof thunder thrown in at times, as well, but there is a lot to \nsettle out in the mass fields before we get concerned about the \ndetails of any convection.",
